'''treflee''' (tref-lē′), ''a.'' [< F. ''tréflé:'' see ''treflé.''] Same as ''treflé.''

'''trefoil''' (trē′foil), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< ME. ''trefoil,'' < OF. ''trifoil, trefeul,'' *''trefle, treffle,'' F. ''trèfle'' = Pr. ''trefueil'' = Sp. Pg. ''trifolio'' = It. ''trifoglio,'' < L. ''trifolium,'' trefoil, lit. three-leaved (sc. ''gramen,'' grass), < ''tres,'' three, + ''folium,'' a leaf: see ''foil''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A plant of the genus ''Trifolium;'' clover. {{smaller|The name is given to various other plants with trifoliolate leaves, in England somewhat specifically to the black medic, ''Medicago lupulina,'' grown for pasture. See ''clover, Stylosanthes,'' and specific names below.}}

{{smaller block|The delicate ''trefoil'' that muffled warm<br>A slope on Ida.
{{right|''T. B. Aldrich,'' Piscataqua River.}}}}

'''2†.''' The third leaf put forth by a young plant.

{{smaller block|To make hem [cabbages] hoor as frost eke crafte is fonde:<br>Let grounden glasse goo sifte on hem aboute,<br>When thaire ''trefoil'' or quaterfoil is oute.
{{right|''Palladius,'' Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 83.}}}}

